[Music]  off-line compared to online retail is  still actually a very large fraction of  consumer spending so although online is  growing quite dramatically as of now  essentially still about 90 percent of  total retail spending happens in the  offline world and firms are still trying  to figure out how to best promote  products how to best run a price  promotions things of that sort and so we  wanted to go back and see can we  actually use tools that we learned about  it in the online world better types of  measurement and can we bring that back  through your flying world and try to  track things better so we know if I run  an ad online  I typically can track did somebody click  on it did it again to go to my website  how much they spend all this linkage is  much closer and those are often things  that we don't observe in the offline  world right so an online retailer would  know how did this consumer get to the  product you're ultimately purchased we  can observe dubrow sing with a lot of  precision offline does usually no  equivalent traditionally we weren't able  to see how did somebody walk through the  store how did they actually get to the  specific aisle where they then picked up  products and so what we do in the study  is we actually bring in a new  measurement tool that's going to mimic  the way we're gonna observe things  online in the offline world and in  particular what we're going to do is  we're gonna rig consumer shopping cards  if you will with so-called radio  frequency identification tags which  worked like a GPS tracker that allow us  to track exactly how does somebody walk  through the store which category is  godigo do they walk past what do they  eventually end up purchasing  [Music]  if in a particular week the supermarket  is running a beer ad do more people buy  a beer  that we were usually able to observe  even before but now we can also see did  actually more people go to the beer  aisle so did did that advertising  attract people to go to different part  was started they wouldn't have otherwise  gone to and then they made the purchase  if that's true then maybe this  advertising will have positive spillover  to other categories maybe people on  their way to the beer aisle are now  gonna pick up salty snacks other things  and so there's sort of anecdotally a lot  of that sense that we are gonna get  these kind of spillover effect so people  often talk about the fact that milk gets  stored in the back of the store  why because people buy it very  frequently and having it in the back of  the store makes them walk past a lot of  other categories and hence they might  pick up other things that they didn't  plan on buying on their way and so you  could imagine that advertising does  something very similar if advertising  allows me to divert traffic to other  parts of the store then maybe products I  pass on that way also gonna end up in my  purchase basket and so that was  something that we weren't usually able  to observe  [Music]  so for a store manager the good news  here is that advertising is quite  effective beyond just individual product  being advertised so what we find is that  when you advertise a particular brand of  beer sales go up in the beer category as  a whole and that increase comes partly  from the advertised brands but also goes  beyond that  so some consumers presumably just pick  up on effect beers being advertised  today they got reminded of I want to buy  beer and hence we seen increase in beer  sales and that's beyond the individual  product being advertised at the same  time we also find that the effect stops  there so to the extent that stores might  have to hope that driving more people to  the beer aisle through advertising will  then spill over into higher sales for  salty snacks or other categories that  are stocked nearby we find no evidence  for that whatsoever so we think so  things stop at the category level but  they go beyond individual product being  advertised and so hence as we think  about allocating advertising budget  thinking about effectiveness at the  level of the category rather than the  more narrow product level or the broader  store level seems to be the right way to  tackle that managerial decision  [Music]

